<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    ==========================================================================================
    PowerFramework.Contracts : THE PUBLISHED CROSS-SERVICE BOUNDARY
    ==========================================================================================

    This project carries NO BEHAVIOUR. It is the boundary definition, not a shared-code back
    door (AAP 0.4.2.3). It owns no *.cs file of its own: every type it produces is generated at
    build time by Grpc.Tools from the *.proto files under Proto/, and the REST half of the
    boundary is the set of OpenAPI documents under OpenApi/.

    WHAT IT PUBLISHES, AND TO WHOM
      Proto/common.v1.proto        shared messages (RetCode, DwBuffer, ItemStatus, DbError,
                                   ConflictDetail)
      Proto/dataservices.v1.proto  C-03 DataWindowService, C-04 ColumnExpressionService
      Proto/persistence.v1.proto   C-05 Query, C-06 Update, C-07 Command, C-08 Transaction
      OpenApi/security.v1.yaml     C-01 TokenService, C-02 CryptoService
      OpenApi/gateway.v1.yaml      C-09 REST ingress, carrying the C-10 health contract

    Consumers reach this project by ProjectReference only. PowerFramework.DataServices and
    PowerFramework.Persistence take the generated SERVER stubs; PowerFramework.Gateway and
    PowerFramework.DataServices take the generated CLIENT stubs; PowerFramework.Security serves
    the OpenAPI-described REST surface; PowerFramework.Contracts.Tests validates the definitions.
    The path and file name are consequently FROZEN: those projects and the root
    PowerFramework.slnx name this file exactly as
    shared/PowerFramework.Contracts/PowerFramework.Contracts.csproj.

    EVERY CONTRACT HERE IS NET NEW, NOT A PORT
    The legacy PowerFramework is a library with no process of its own, so no contract translates
    an existing wire format. pfw.pbt declares a single flat ordered LibList of 32 libraries and
    PowerBuilder resolves one global namespace by that ORDER, which means there are no
    namespaces, no import statements, no serialization layer and no route table to rewrite
    (AAP 0.4.5.1). Nothing here is a translation; it is a genuine creation.

    LEGACY BUILD REFERENCES, READ FOR INTENT ONLY (constraint C-C)
    ws_objects/pfw.pbl.src/project.srj and ws_objects/pfw.pbl.src/p_pfw.srj are the legacy
    PowerBuilder build definitions. Neither is usable as a template, and their contradictions are
    verified rather than assumed: 29 PBD lines against 28, COM:Appeon against COM:Sybase, Inc.,
    only the former lists pfw.utility.sqlite.pbl, the latter embeds developer-workstation paths
    under F:\pfw\ together with a retired library, and BOTH name pfw.utility.imgcodec.pbl, which
    exists nowhere in the repository, so neither would build as written (AAP 0.8.6 R5). The .NET
    build is therefore authored clean and validated by execution. Those files, pfw.pbt and
    pfwx.pbt are never edited, reformatted or moved, and no item glob below reaches into the
    read-only legacy tree.

    INHERITED, SO DELIBERATELY NOT RESTATED HERE
    Directory.Build.props at the repository root supplies TargetFramework net10.0, LangVersion,
    Nullable, ImplicitUsings, TreatWarningsAsErrors, the .NET analyzers and
    ManagePackageVersionsCentrally. Directory.Packages.props is the SOLE version authority.
    Restating any of those would create two places to change one setting and would undermine the
    directory walk-up inheritance that makes the documented per-service build command work from a
    clean checkout (AAP 0.3.2.1). Every PackageReference below is consequently VERSIONLESS.

    DELIBERATE ABSENCES, each a constraint rather than an oversight
      C-A  ZERO ProjectReference. In particular there is no reference to
           PowerFramework.Shared.Kernel even though Proto/common.v1.proto's RetCode values must
           equal RetCode.cs's values: that agreement is a review-time invariant with no
           compile-time edge, and adding the reference would turn a boundary definition into a
           shared-code path.
      C-A  No Compile item, no analyzer and no source generator of this project's own. Generated
           Protobuf and gRPC code lands under obj/ and is compiled automatically.
      C-B  Exactly three package references. Grpc.Tools and Google.Protobuf arrive transitively
           through Grpc.AspNetCore and must not be referenced explicitly (AAP 0.5.1), and
           Grpc.AspNetCore.Server.Reflection, Swashbuckle.AspNetCore and Scalar.AspNetCore are
           deliberately excluded (AAP 0.5.3).
      C-D  No deferred capability is anticipated here. This project holds no item, property,
           condition or commented-out placeholder awaiting a Phase-2 service, and no contract
           definition for one; AAP 0.2.2.2 names the four that are out of scope, and in this
           system they surface only as Gateway's reserved 501 routes.
      C-I  Every glob is project-relative with forward slashes and no parent-directory segment,
           so the project restores identically from its own directory and from the repository
           root, which is the build context of every service Dockerfile.
      No secret, key, connection string or token appears here in any form.

    RULES POSITION
    review_rules returns exactly "No user rules provided.", so no user-specified rule governs
    this file. The enterprise-standard baseline of AAP 0.7.2 applies in its place and is honoured
    above: warnings as errors inherited and never relaxed, central package management with no
    floating version, a clean dependency audit at restore, no secret in source, and explicitly
    versioned contracts as the only permitted cross-service coupling.
    ==========================================================================================
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- Project-local values only; everything else is inherited from Directory.Build.props. -->
    <RootNamespace>PowerFramework.Contracts</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>PowerFramework.Contracts</AssemblyName>
    <!-- Consumed exclusively by ProjectReference inside this repository, never pushed to a feed. -->
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Package references : exactly three, all versionless">

    <!--
      gRPC server and client support, and the Grpc.Tools build integration that compiles the
      Protobuf items below. Grpc.Tools and Google.Protobuf arrive transitively (Google.Protobuf
      resolves to 3.31.1 because Grpc.AspNetCore 2.83.0 constrains it), so referencing either
      explicitly would be scope creep against C-B and would risk pinning a conflicting version.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Grpc.AspNetCore" />

    <!--
      OpenAPI document generation and object model for the REST half of the boundary.

      NOTE FOR CONSUMING SERVICES, measured on this toolchain rather than assumed. This package
      ships build/Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi.targets, which is what sets InterceptorsNamespaces
      for its XML-comment source generator, and it ships no buildTransitive folder. Those targets
      therefore apply only to a project that references the package DIRECTLY, while the generator
      itself does flow onward through a ProjectReference. A service that calls AddOpenApi() while
      relying on this project's reference instead of declaring its own consequently fails with
      CS9137 ("the interceptors feature is not enabled in this namespace"). The fix is the
      reference the plan already prescribes, never an InterceptorsNamespaces workaround: Gateway,
      Security and the DataServices REST projection each reference Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi
      directly (AAP 0.5.1). Both real shapes were verified to build warning-clean against this
      project: a web service with the direct reference plus AddOpenApi(), and one with neither,
      which is the Persistence shape.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi" />

    <!--
      MANDATORY DIRECT REFERENCE, not a preference (AAP 0.5.2). Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi
      10.0.10 pulls Microsoft.OpenApi 2.0.0 transitively, which raises the high-severity NuGet
      audit advisory NU1903. This direct reference is the mechanism by which central package
      management overrides that transitive version with the 2.11.0 pin. Advancing to the 3.x line
      instead BREAKS the build: the 10.0.x OpenAPI source generator is compiled against the 2.x
      object model and emits two CS0200 "cannot assign, read only" errors from the SDK's own
      generated XML-comment support file. 2.11.0 is the highest published 2.x and the only value
      that is simultaneously non-vulnerable and compatible. If NU1903 ever reappears, restore this
      reference; never suppress the advisory in its place.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.OpenApi" />

  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Protobuf contracts : the gRPC half of the published boundary">

    <!--
      ProtoRoot IS THE CONTRACT WITH THE Proto/ FOLDER, so it is stated explicitly instead of
      being left to Grpc.Tools' default. ProtoRoot is "Proto", therefore imports are BARE FILE
      NAMES relative to that root: dataservices.v1.proto and persistence.v1.proto both write

          import "common.v1.proto";

      and never "Proto/common.v1.proto". Grpc.Tools' own _Protobuf_SetProtoRoot target defaults
      the root to the project directory when the metadata is absent, in which case every import
      would need a Proto/ prefix; the mismatch surfaces as a confusing protoc "file not found".
      Verified empirically on SDK 10.0.302 with a bare-file-name import across two proto files.

      GrpcServices="Both" is required, not merely convenient: this single project serves the
      generated SERVER base classes to DataServices (C-03, C-04) and Persistence (C-05 through
      C-08) AND the generated CLIENT stubs to Gateway and DataServices. Either "Server" or
      "Client" alone would break half the consumers.

      One item covers the whole folder, nested subdirectories included. Forward slashes keep
      evaluation identical on Linux, the target operating system, and on Windows. Grpc.Tools
      leaves EnableDefaultProtobufItems false by default, so this is the only source of Protobuf
      items and there is no duplicate-item hazard.

      The None Remove serves the same purpose as the one in the OpenAPI group below: the SDK's
      default None glob also matches these files, and removing them there leaves each .proto
      carrying the Protobuf build action and nothing else.
    -->
    <None Remove="Proto/**/*.proto" />
    <Protobuf Include="Proto/**/*.proto" ProtoRoot="Proto" GrpcServices="Both" />

  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="OpenAPI contracts : the REST half of the published boundary">

    <!--
      ITEM TYPE DECISION: EmbeddedResource, chosen over None with CopyToOutputDirectory.
      These documents are the source of truth for C-01, C-02, C-09 and C-10, so they must never
      be invisible to the build. Embedding is preferred over copying because
        1. the documents travel inside PowerFramework.Contracts.dll and therefore survive the
           multi-stage Dockerfile publish boundary, where a loose copied file can be dropped;
        2. consumers and PowerFramework.Contracts.Tests read them with no file-path or
           AppContext.BaseDirectory assumption, on any operating system;
        3. the specification versions atomically with the assembly that carries it, which is
           exactly the "explicitly versioned contracts as the only cross-service coupling"
           baseline of AAP 0.7.2.
      Logical resource names are the SDK default and therefore deterministic: RootNamespace plus
      the relative directory with separators replaced by dots. OpenApi/gateway.v1.yaml is read as
      "PowerFramework.Contracts.OpenApi.gateway.v1.yaml", and Assembly.GetManifestResourceNames
      on this assembly enumerates the full set. Verified on SDK 10.0.302 by a downstream project
      reading the document back through GetManifestResourceStream.

      The None Remove is what leaves each document carrying exactly one build action. The SDK's
      default None glob is <None Include="**/*" /> less source files and .resx, so it already
      matches OpenApi/**/*.yaml; removing them there first means each document is an
      EmbeddedResource and nothing else. The rejected alternative, for the record, was
      <None Include="OpenApi/**/*.yaml" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />: measured on
      this toolchain it re-includes what the default glob already carries and leaves a duplicate
      None item in the evaluated project, and it publishes the document as a loose file that then
      has to survive the container image boundary intact.
    -->
    <None Remove="OpenApi/**/*.yaml" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="OpenApi/**/*.yaml" />

  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
